nike is the largest sports company in  the world by far in fact they're so  dominant that they're five times larger  than the second largest sports brand in  the world adidas coming in at 262  billion dollars this means that nike is  larger than even toyota and coca-cola  while the magnitude of their success is  super impressive it's not too crazy when  you consider that nike shoes and  clothing are basically ubiquitous but  despite the ubiquitous nature of nike  most people have no clue who's behind  the company this is actually quite  ironic given that phil knight's tenure  at nike is longer than the entire  existence of apple and microsoft on top  of this phil is the 19th richest person  in the world with a net worth of 62  billion dollars so here's the story of  the mystery man behind nike  taking a look back phil knight was born  on february 24 1938 in portland oregon  phil's father bill was originally a  lawyer by trade but he had since opted  to run his own newspaper publishing  company called the oregon journal bill  was quite a strict man who cared a lot  about hard work and discipline he didn't  like the idea of handouts and thought  that everyone needed to fend for  themselves this was made quite clear  when he refused to give phil's summer  job at the oregon journal  since phil couldn't get a job at his  father's company he decided to apply for  a job at oregonian who was his father's  biggest rival  at oregonian phil worked the morning  shift where he tabulated sports scores  the job wasn't too bad but the same  could not be said about the commute phil  had to run a full seven miles each way  to get to work and back fortunately  though phil wasn't just a great runner  but he was a world class runner his  personal best at the mile was just 4  minutes and 13 seconds  moving into college phil would attend  the university of oregon in eugene where  he was a star athlete in 1957 58 and 59  phil would win varsity letters for his  track performance and he would get the  opportunity to be trained by the  legendary bill boerman throughout his  career bill would train 31 olympic  athletes 22 ncaa champions and 16 sub 4  minute milers so boro men knew what he  was doing similar to phil's father  boermann pushed phil to always challenge  the limits and never settle for average  though it was easy for phil to apply  these principles to running it wasn't so  easy with academics  it's not that phil was a bad student or  anything he was just confused as to what  he wanted to do  he had been majoring in journalism  probably inspired by his father but he  was having second doubts about whether  that's what he really wanted to do  so after graduation in 1959 phil went  ahead and joined the army he completed a  year of service before he decided that  he wanted to return to school enrolling  in stanford's mba program while  completing his mba phil would write a  paper called can japanese sport shoes do  to german sports shoes what japanese  cameras did the german cameras and this  would become the blueprint for nike  phil didn't just jump fully into selling  shoes after college though  instead it felt opted to take the cpa  exam and join a local accounting firm in  portland in the background though phil  was hard at work testing out different  designs and securing contractors in  november of 1962 phil traveled to japan  where he discovered something called the  tiger banshu manufactured by ononsuka  phil was thoroughly impressed by the  high quality and low cost of the shoes  and would form a licensing deal with the  company after he returned to the u.s  phil would reach out to his former coach  bill boroman to get his opinion on the  shoot and to feel surprised bill not  only loved the shoes but he was willing  to join the business as a partner phil  of course took bill up on that offer and  the duo started blue ribbon sports in  january of 1964. neither phil nor bill  had any high level business connections  or enough money to market their product  so they decided to market their shoes  the old-fashioned way the duo started  off by attending every track meet in the  area and attempting to sell their shoes  to the athletes this was moderately  successful and they were able to sell  1300 pairs by the end of the first year  grossing a total of eight thousand  dollars worth of sales in their second  year of business the duo onboarded a  full-time employee who helped them  attend more track meets and over double  sales to twenty thousand dollars and  this gave them the confidence to open up  a physical location in santa monica  california by 1967 sales were doing so  well that phil would open another store  on the east coast in wellesley  massachusetts about two years into  running both stores phil would finally  quit his accounting job and go all in on  the business this seemed like a great  decision for two years but in 1971 the  company would run into a mass roadblock  they had a dispute with their  manufacturer or tsuka and the two would  choose to go separate ways given that  blue ribbon sports no longer had the  rights to sell onotzuka's shoes phil and  bill were forced to create their own  shoes and that brings us to nike  given phil's experience as an athlete  himself he decided to design the shoes  based on what he would want as a runner  he also consulted with other runners  such as long-distance runners steve  preventing to get feedback on the shoes  and improve the design  most news sources report that it was  phil who designed the company's first  shoes but an olympic athlete named ogus  davis suggests that it was actually  built  otis was one of the first people to try  the new shoes and he says that he  physically witnessed bill make the new  shoes with a waffle iron in terms of his  experience with the shoes body says that  he didn't particularly like how they  felt on his feet as the shoes had no  support and they were too tight so  clearly bill and phil had some work to  do but they would figure it out over the  next few years in terms of branding for  the shoe the duo didn't want to use the  blue ribbon sports brand as it wasn't  that catchy or memorable  when they were brainstorming new brand  ideas an employee named jeff johnson  would suggest the name nike which is the  name of the greek winged goddess of  victory around the same time phil  reached out to a graphic design student  named carolyn davidson and he paid 35 to  create a new logo for the new brand  carolyn came up with a couple of logo  designs but phil wasn't a fan of any of  them carolyn offered to modify the  designs and come up with new designs as  well but phil didn't want to delay the  manufacturing process any further  so he simply settled with the swoosh  logo stating that maybe it'll grow on  him ironically the swoosh logo has  turned into one of the most iconic and  recognizable logos in the world  anyway nike's first track shoe the  cortez made its debut as a 1972 olympic  trials and it didn't take long for the  shoe to spread like wildfire  runners around the world wanted to try  out this new shoe put out by bill  boerman and once they tried it they were  hooked  within just eight years nike went from  launching their first shoe to  controlling 50 of the atlantic shoe  market nike became such a large portion  of blue ribbon sports that phil decided  to rename the company itself nike in  1978 and in 1980 phil would take the  company public  bill slowly cashed out throughout the  1980s and started to step away from the  business as he was getting quite old but  phil was just getting started and his  next order of business was securing  endorsements one of nike's first  endorsement deals was with the tennis  superstar john mcenroe john had recently  heard his ankle and was taking some time  off but when he finally returned he was  wearing nike shoes which exploded the  popularity of nike amongst the tennis  world up until then nike shoes were  mainly purchased by runners but after  this positive experience with the jon  phil realized that he could use other  sports stars to break into those sports  as well this of course leads us to the  partnerships with michael jordan andre  iguazi charles barkley and tiger woods  in the 1990s nike expanded into hockey  golf and soccer and by the end of the  decade they would achieve over 10  billion dollars in annual sales  unfortunately bill passed away at the  end of 1999 right before the turn of the  century but there's no doubt that he  lived a solid life stretching 88 years  anyway nike was also making several  acquisitions to aid in their growth for  example in 1988 they bought cole haan  which they would eventually sell in  2012. similarly they bought bower in  1994 and sold it in 2008  but by far their most famous acquisition  didn't come to 2003 when they purchased  converse moving into the new century  nike has placed a strong emphasis on  sports technology they started selling  heart rate monitors high altitude risk  compasses they partnered with companies  like apple all of this has secured  nike's position as the dominant sports  player in the new tech-filled world but  the same cannot be said about phil  knight  there's no question that phil is quite a  private man who avoids his spotlight as  much as possible i'm sure his value for  privacy plays a large role in this but i  think there are also some other factors  driving him away from the public's view  starting with nepotism unlike phil's  father who refused to give phil a job at  his own company phil goes out of his way  to help his children through their  careers take his son travis for example  after an unsuccessful attempt at  becoming a rapper travis decided to  become an animator  in 1998 phil would buy 15 of an  animation studio named vincent studios  and travis would get a job there shortly  after as an animator  a few years later phil would buy the  entire studio rename it leica and  appoint travis to the new board on top  of this phil would invest 180 million  dollars into the company to help them  produce their first stop-motion film  called coraline and after the launch of  the film travis would become the new ceo  and president of the company i don't  know about you but it looks to me like  phil uses influence and money to  transition travis from being a failed  rapper to being the ceo of an animation  studio now of course phil didn't break  any laws or do anything illegal but this  isn't particularly a good look aside  from nepotism phil doesn't have the best  of records when it comes to human rights  since the 1990s nike has been pummeled  with human rights concerns and poor  treatment of employees there are reports  suggesting that nike workers in vietnam  were earning just 61 to 89 cents per  hour in 2016 and the use of corporal  punishment is allegedly common in their  factories here in the us many activists  are complaining that amazon doesn't pay  enough and let's not forget amazon has a  minimum wage of 15 per hour for all u.s  employees yet jeff bezos is one of the  most hated billionaires out there  compared to jeff bezos phil knight's  popularity doesn't even make it onto the  charts imagine if phil was as popular as  jeff he'd be torched alive by the media  for paying employees less than a dollar  per hour and i think we'd agree that  it's better to not be known at all than  to have a negative public perception  like jeff bezos aside from paying low  wages and nepotism bill is also  notorious for extreme lobbying efforts  over the years phil has spent millions  of dollars on political donations to  republican candidates in oregon  similarly he was the single largest  donor for the campaign against oregon  ballot measures 66 and 67. this  legislation was supposed to increase  income tax on corporations and  high-income individuals now i'm not  saying that phil should pay more taxes  or that he's wrong for supporting the  republican party  the fundamental problem isn't the  policies that he's supporting the  fundamental problem is that he's using  his money to influence the government  and i think both sides can agree then  this is wrong  considering all this maybe phil's  relatively small public image isn't due  to his modesty or privacy but because he  doesn't want to attract attention  towards his frowned upon activities but  that's just speculation why do you guys  think phil is so private comment that  down below also drop a like if you're a  fan of nike's products but wish that  their business was a lot cleaner and of  course consider joining our discord  community to suggest your video ideas  and consider subscribing to see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm hari and i'll see you guys on  the next one  [Music]  you

